---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="FSR Settings Guide for DroidDeck — DroidDeck Guide"
  description="Recommended AMD FSR settings for DroidDeck. Balance image quality and performance on Android handhelds and Snapdragon phones."
  page="games"

  locale="en"
>
  <style>
    .page-header{background:var(--night);color:white;padding:90px 0 50px}
    .page-header h1{font-size:36px;margin-bottom:12px}
    .page-header p{color:#93a7ba;max-width:600px}
    .page-content{padding:50px 0 80px;max-width:820px}
    .guide-section{margin-bottom:42px}
    .guide-section h2{font-size:24px;margin-bottom:14px}
    .guide-section p{font-size:15px;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.9}
    .guide-section ul{padding-left:22px;font-size:15px;color:#43576c;line-height:1.9}
    .guide-section li{margin:10px 0}
    .fsr-table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;overflow:hidden;font-size:14px;margin-top:16px}
    .fsr-table th,.fsr-table td{padding:14px 16px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);text-align:left;vertical-align:top}
    .fsr-table th{background:#f4f7fa;font-size:11px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.04em;color:var(--muted)}
    .fsr-table tr:last-child td{border-bottom:none}
    .quality{font-weight:700;color:#16a34a}
    .balanced{font-weight:700;color:#d97706}
    .performance{font-weight:700;color:#dc2626}
  </style>
  
    <section class="page-header">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="hero-label"><span></span> Game Compatibility</div>
        <h1>FSR Settings Guide</h1>
        <p>How to use AMD FSR to squeeze more performance out of DroidDeck without making games look too soft.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="page-content wrap">
      <div class="guide-section">
        <h2>What FSR mode means</h2>
        <p>FSR renders the game at a lower internal resolution, then upscales it. Lower modes give more FPS but softer image quality.</p>
        <table class="fsr-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Mode</th>
              <th>Upscale ratio</th>
              <th>Use when</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="quality">Quality</span></td>
              <td>1.5× (67% render)</td>
              <td>You have headroom and want the sharpest image.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="balanced">Balanced</span></td>
              <td>1.7× (59% render)</td>
              <td>Best mix of quality and performance for most games.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="performance">Performance</span></td>
              <td>2.0× (50% render)</td>
              <td>You need every frame; image will be noticeably softer.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="performance">Ultra Performance</span></td>
              <td>3.0× (33% render)</td>
              <td>Last resort for very heavy games on weak devices.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="guide-section">
        <h2>Recommended presets by game type</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>2D indie games:</strong> No FSR needed, or Quality if you want to save battery.</li>
          <li><strong>Light 3D games (Hades, Dead Cells):</strong> Quality or Balanced at 1080p.</li>
          <li><strong>Open-world 3A (Witcher 3, GTAV):</strong> Balanced or Performance at 720p.</li>
          <li><strong>Struggling 3A (Elden Ring, Sekiro):</strong> Performance at 720p; Ultra Performance only if playable otherwise.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="guide-section">
        <h2>Tips</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Always combine FSR with a lower in-game resolution, not just FSR alone.</li>
          <li>On small phone screens, Performance mode looks better than on a large monitor.</li>
          <li>If the image looks too soft, try increasing sharpening in-game or use Quality instead.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
  
  
</Layout>
